# 09. Feature-First Organization

AEGIS should be organized primarily by domain capability rather than by generic file type.

Prefer:

```text
features/threats/
```

over:

```text
components/threatButtons/
components/threatCards/
components/threatTables/
```

The feature owns the composition of its interface.

Shared components remain genuinely shared.

---

# 10. Shared Components

Shared components include things such as:

```text
Button
Input
Select
Dialog
Drawer
Tabs
Table
Badge
Tooltip
Skeleton
EmptyState
ErrorState
Timeline
MetricCard
StatusIndicator
CommandPanel
```

They must not contain AEGIS-specific business logic unless explicitly intended.

---

# 11. Domain Components

Domain-specific components include:

```text
ThreatStatus
ThreatConfidence
RiskIndicator
InvestigationTimeline
ContainmentAction
TransmissionPathway
RecoveryAssessment
ResilienceGap
```

These components may understand AEGIS concepts.

---

# 12. Screen Components

Screen components compose features into complete operational experiences.

Example:

```text
GlobalMonitoringScreen
ThreatIntelligenceScreen
InvestigationScreen
ContainmentScreen
RecoveryScreen
```

A screen should orchestrate.

It should not become a 2,000-line monolith.

---

# 13. Layout Architecture

AEGIS uses a persistent application shell.

Conceptually:

```text
┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ GLOBAL HEADER                                 │
├────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┤
│            │                                  │
│ SIDEBAR    │       PRIMARY CONTENT            │
│            │                                  │
│            │                                  │
├────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┤
│ OPTIONAL CONTEXT / STATUS REGION              │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The shell remains stable while operational context changes.

---

# 14. Global Shell Responsibilities

The shell manages:

```text
navigation
current location/context
user identity
organization context
system status
global notifications
application-level alerts
theme/design tokens
responsive behavior
```

It must not own individual threat state.

---

# 15. Navigation Architecture

Navigation must represent the operational structure of AEGIS.

It should allow the user to move between:

```text
monitoring
threat intelligence
investigation
risk
response
recovery
preparedness
research
```

without losing context.

---

# 16. Deep Linking

Every meaningful operational entity should be addressable.

Example:

```text
/threats/THR-001
/investigations/INV-001
/cases/CAS-001
/containment-actions/CNT-001
```

Deep links must restore the relevant application context.

---

# 17. URL as Navigation State

The URL should represent durable navigational state such as:

```text
entity
tab
filter
search
time range
location
```

Temporary UI state should remain local.

---

# 18. Browser Navigation

Back and forward navigation must behave predictably.

If a user moves:

```text
Monitoring
→ Threat
→ Investigation
→ Finding
```

then browser back should return through those meaningful contexts.

---

# 19. Route Protection

Protected operational routes must require authentication.

Authorization must be enforced by the backend.

Frontend route protection exists for:

```text
UX
navigation control
early rejection
```

not as the authoritative security layer.

---

# 20. Route Registry

The application must maintain one authoritative route registry.

Conceptually:

```text
AEGIS_ROUTES
```

This prevents route strings from being duplicated throughout the codebase.

---

# 21. 13-Screen Integration

The 13 screens defined in Document 04 are the authoritative product screen registry.

The frontend architecture must implement exactly those screens.

No additional "helpful" screens should be invented during implementation.

If a required capability does not fit an existing screen:

```text
STOP
↓
identify architectural gap
↓
update specification
↓
then implement
```

---

# 22. Screen Contract

Every screen must define:

```text
route
purpose
primary user
required queries
available commands
required permissions
loading state
empty state
error state
success state
real-time events
navigation destinations
```

The screen implementation must follow Document 04.

---

# 23. Screen Composition Pattern

A screen should follow:

```text
Screen
 ↓
Page Controller / Feature Orchestrator
 ↓
Feature Sections
 ↓
Domain Components
 ↓
Shared UI Components
```

Avoid:

```text
Screen
 ↓
400 unrelated components
 ↓
random API calls
 ↓
local state everywhere
```

---

# 24. Data Fetching Architecture

Data fetching must be centralized through the data-access layer.

Conceptually:

```text
Screen
 ↓
Query Hook / Query Function
 ↓
API Client
 ↓
API
```

Components should not independently fetch the same entity through arbitrary methods.

---

# 25. Server State vs Client State

AEGIS must distinguish:

## Server State

Data owned by the backend.

Examples:

```text
threat
risk
investigation
case
finding
containment
response
recovery
preparedness
```

## Client State

Temporary interface state.

Examples:

```text
drawerOpen
selectedTab
hoveredPoint
temporaryFilter
modalState
draftText
```

---

# 26. Global State

Global state should be minimal.

Appropriate examples:

```text
authenticated user
organization context
application preferences
navigation context
connection status
global notification state
```

Do not put every piece of server data into one giant global store.

---

# 27. Server State Management

A query/caching layer should own remote data.

A suitable implementation may use a mature server-state library rather than building custom caching.

The architecture should support:

```text
query caching
request deduplication
refetch
mutation handling
stale state
optimistic behavior where safe
invalidation
reconciliation
```

---

# 28. No Fake Global State

Do not create:

```text
globalThreatState
globalRiskState
globalInvestigationState
```

simply because multiple screens display them.

The authoritative source remains the API.

---

# 29. Command Architecture

User actions must flow through application commands.

Example:

```text
User clicks:
"Authorize Containment"

↓
UI command handler

↓
authorizeContainment()

↓
API client

↓
POST command

↓
Backend validation

↓
Result

↓
State reconciliation
```

---

# 30. Command Functions

Commands should have meaningful names.

Examples:

```text
createThreat()
startInvestigation()
finalizeRiskAssessment()
proposeContainment()
authorizeContainment()
startContainment()
verifyContainment()
startRecoveryAssessment()
confirmRecovery()
createPreparednessLesson()
```

Avoid generic functions such as:

```text
updateData()
changeStatus()
saveThing()
```

---

# 31. Mutation States

Every command must expose:

```text
idle
submitting
success
error
```

For consequential commands, the UI should additionally handle:

```text
stale
unauthorized
invalidTransition
conflict
timeout
```

---

# 32. No False Success

The UI must never show:

```text
"Containment authorized"
```

because the user clicked the button.

It may show:

```text
"Authorizing..."
```

until the backend confirms success.

Only authoritative confirmation can produce the final success state.

---

# 33. Optimistic UI

Optimistic updates are dangerous for consequential operational state.

Default:

```text
NO OPTIMISTIC STATE TRANSITION
```

for:

```text
threat classification
risk status
containment
response
recovery
authorization
```

Use optimistic behavior only where it cannot misrepresent operational truth.

---

# 34. Loading Architecture

Every data-dependent section must define a loading state.

Possible patterns:

```text
skeleton
progressive loading
placeholder metric
loading indicator
```

Do not display fabricated values while data is loading.

---

# 35. Empty State Architecture

Empty does not mean error.

Example:

```text
No active investigations
```

is different from:

```text
Investigations could not be loaded
```

The interface must distinguish:

```text
empty
loading
error
unavailable
```

---

# 36. Error Architecture

Errors exist at:

```text
component
feature
screen
application
network
authentication
authorization
domain
```

The UI should display the smallest useful recovery surface.

---

# 37. Error Recovery

Where possible:

```text
Retry
Refresh
Return to previous context
Open related entity
```

should be available.

Do not simply show:

```text
Something went wrong.
```

for every failure.

---

# 38. Domain Errors in UI

Backend errors such as:

```text
INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION
STALE_ENTITY_VERSION
ACTION_NOT_AUTHORIZED
MISSING_REQUIRED_EVIDENCE
```

must map to human-readable operational explanations.

The UI should preserve the underlying machine-readable error for logging and diagnostics.

---

# 39. Real-Time Architecture

AEGIS requires real-time awareness.

The frontend should conceptually use:

```text
REST / HTTP
+
REAL-TIME EVENT STREAM
```

HTTP handles:

```text
queries
commands
```

Real-time transport handles:

```text
events
```

---

# 40. Real-Time Event Flow

```text
Backend state change
 ↓
Domain event
 ↓
Realtime transport
 ↓
Frontend event handler
 ↓
Query invalidation / cache update
 ↓
UI reconciliation
```

---

# 41. Real-Time Is Not a Second Database

The event stream must not become the sole source of truth.

If a client receives:

```text
THREAT_STATUS_UPDATED
```

it may update or invalidate local state.

For critical information, it must remain possible to reconcile against the API.

---

# 42. Connection State

The global application must represent:

```text
CONNECTED
CONNECTING
DISCONNECTED
RECONNECTING
DEGRADED
```

where appropriate.

---

# 43. Degraded Mode

If real-time connectivity fails:

```text
AEGIS should remain usable where safe.
```

The interface should clearly indicate stale/reduced freshness.

It must not pretend that real-time information is current.

---

# 44. Freshness Indicators

Operational information should communicate freshness where relevant.

Examples:

```text
Updated 12 seconds ago
Live
Delayed
Connection interrupted
Last synchronized 3 minutes ago
```

Do not use "LIVE" merely as decoration.

It must correspond to actual system behavior.

---

# 45. Polling

Polling may be used where:

```text
real-time transport is unavailable
data changes are infrequent
operational freshness requirements permit it
```

Polling intervals must not be chosen arbitrarily.

---

# 46. Forms Architecture

Forms should be schema-driven where practical.

Each form must define:

```text
fields
types
requiredness
validation
default values
submission command
success behavior
error behavior
permissions
```

---

# 47. Draft State

Forms may maintain local draft state.

Drafts are not authoritative domain state until submitted and accepted.

---

# 48. Confirmation Architecture

High-consequence actions require explicit confirmation where defined by product requirements.

Example:

```text
Authorize Containment
```

should clearly communicate:

```text
what will happen
target
current state
user authority
consequence
```

before execution where appropriate.

---

# 49. Action Hierarchy

The visual hierarchy should distinguish:

```text
view
inspect
analyze
recommend
authorize
execute
verify
```

These are not equivalent actions.

---

# 50. Preventing Accidental Actions

High-consequence controls should not be:

```text
tiny
ambiguous
hidden
adjacent to destructive alternatives
triggered by accidental clicks
```

The interface must reduce operational error.

---

# 51. Modal vs Drawer

Use:

## Drawer

For contextual inspection.

## Modal

For focused decisions requiring attention.

## Dedicated screen

For complex workflows.

Do not put an entire operational workflow inside a modal simply to avoid creating a proper route.

---

# 52. Tables

Tables are appropriate for:

```text
events
signals
threats
investigations
cases
evidence
actions
```

They must support the specific requirements defined in Document 04.

---

# 53. Table Architecture

A table should separate:

```text
data
columns
sorting
filtering
selection
pagination
row actions
```

from the visual table component.

---

# 54. Data Visualization

Visualizations must communicate operational meaning.

Use:

```text
trend
relationship
geography
distribution
timeline
status
risk
```

only when they improve decision making.

Avoid decorative charts.

---

# 55. Three.js / 3D Usage

Three.js may be used for the AEGIS Living Map or spatial visualization where defined.

It must not be used merely because:

```text
"3D looks futuristic."
```

The visualization must provide operational value.

---

# 56. Living Map Architecture

The map should be treated as a visualization layer over authoritative location and event data.

Conceptually:

```text
Location Data
+
Event Data
+
Threat Data
+
Risk Data
+
Spatial Rules
↓
Visualization Model
↓
Three.js
```

Three.js must not become the source of operational state.

---

# 57. Map Interaction

Map interactions may change:

```text
selected location
selected entity
viewport
layer visibility
filter context
```

They must not directly mutate domain state unless an explicit command is triggered.

---

# 58. Motion Architecture

Motion should communicate:

```text
transition
state change
attention
hierarchy
spatial relationship
```

not:

```text
entertainment
gaming
decoration
```

---

# 59. Animation Rules

Animations must never obscure:

```text
critical information
status
controls
warnings
time-sensitive updates
```

Motion should degrade gracefully on lower-powered devices.

---

# 60. Design System Integration

The frontend consumes the AEGIS design system.

The design system should define:

```text
tokens
typography
spacing
color
surface
borders
radii
shadows
icons
status semantics
components
motion
```

The frontend must not invent one-off colors or typography values inside individual screens.

---

# 61. Visual Direction

The visual system remains:

```text
Deep Obsidian
Surgical White
Antigen Amber
```

with restrained supporting semantic colors where required.

The visual goal is:

> **Clinical Authority.**

Not:

```text
gaming HUD
cyberpunk
military game
sci-fi dashboard cosplay
```

---

# 62. Information Density

AEGIS is a high-density operational interface.

Density must come from:

```text
hierarchy
alignment
grouping
compact typography
efficient tables
controlled spacing
progressive disclosure
```

not from:

```text
tiny unreadable text
random cards
excessive borders
visual noise
```

---

# 63. Accessibility

Accessibility is part of system reliability.

The frontend must support:

```text
keyboard navigation
visible focus
semantic HTML
screen-reader compatibility
sufficient contrast
reduced motion
clear status communication
non-color-dependent meaning
```

---

# 64. Color Semantics

Color must not be the only way information is communicated.

For example:

```text
Amber + icon + label
```

rather than:

```text
Amber alone
```

This is especially important for alerts and risk states.

---

# 65. Responsive Architecture

AEGIS is primarily a professional operational application.

The layout should prioritize:

```text
desktop
large desktop
operational workstation
```

while maintaining reasonable behavior on smaller screens.

Do not design the desktop experience by simply shrinking it until it fits mobile.

---

# 66. Performance Architecture

The frontend must avoid unnecessary rendering.

Important strategies include:

```text
component boundaries
memoization where justified
virtualized large datasets
lazy loading
code splitting
image optimization
controlled subscriptions
efficient map rendering
```

Do not optimize blindly.

Measure first where possible.

---

# 67. Large Dataset Rendering

Potentially large datasets such as:

```text
events
observations
signals
audit records
```

must not render thousands of DOM nodes simultaneously.

Use:

```text
pagination
virtualization
server-side filtering
server-side sorting
```

where appropriate.

---

# 68. Map Performance

The Living Map must consider:

```text
number of entities
number of layers
update frequency
animation cost
GPU capability
device memory
```

Real-time updates must not force the entire map to rerender unnecessarily.

---

# 69. Data Prefetching

Prefetching is permitted when the next navigation target is predictable.

Example:

```text
Threat list
 ↓
hover/select threat
 ↓
prefetch threat detail
```

But prefetching must not create unnecessary operational traffic.

---

# 70. Cache Invalidation Strategy

After mutation:

```text
mutation success
 ↓
identify affected entities
 ↓
invalidate/update relevant queries
 ↓
process event
 ↓
render authoritative state
```

Avoid refreshing the entire application after every command.

---

# 71. Query Keys

Query keys must be deterministic.

Conceptually:

```text
["threat", threatId]

["threats", filters]

["investigation", investigationId]

["investigations", filters]
```

Do not build ad hoc cache identifiers throughout components.

---

# 72. Permission Architecture

Frontend permissions should be derived from authenticated application context.

Example:

```text
canViewThreat
canAssessRisk
canStartInvestigation
canAuthorizeContainment
canExecuteContainment
canConfirmRecovery
```

These are UI capabilities.

The backend remains authoritative.

---

# 73. Permission-Aware UI

A user who lacks permission should receive appropriate behavior:

```text
hidden
disabled
read-only
explanation
```

based on the product requirement.

Do not hide critical system context merely because the user cannot act on it.

---

# 74. Read-Only Mode

AEGIS should support read-only contexts where required.

In read-only mode:

```text
information remains visible
commands are unavailable
state remains inspectable
```

---

# 75. Audit-Aware UI

Consequential actions should expose appropriate traceability.

Where relevant:

```text
performed by
performed at
reason
previous state
current state
```

The UI does not create the audit record manually.

The backend does.

---

# 76. Notification Architecture

Notifications should reference actual events.

Examples:

```text
New high-priority anomaly
Threat classification updated
Investigation assigned
Containment authorization required
Recovery assessment completed
```

Avoid generic notification spam.

---

# 77. Notification Severity

Notifications should distinguish:

```text
informational
attention
warning
critical
```

according to defined semantics.

Do not mark everything critical.

---

# 78. Search Architecture

Global search should provide structured navigation across approved entities.

Potential entities:

```text
threat
event
investigation
case
finding
location
evidence
```

Search results must indicate entity type.

---

# 79. Search Result Contract

Conceptually:

```text
{
  "id": "...",
  "type": "threat",
  "title": "...",
  "status": "...",
  "context": "..."
}
```

The frontend then navigates to the canonical route.

---

# 80. Application Lifecycle

AEGIS frontend lifecycle:

```text
BOOT
 ↓
LOAD CONFIG
 ↓
AUTHENTICATE
 ↓
LOAD USER CONTEXT
 ↓
LOAD ORGANIZATION CONTEXT
 ↓
INITIALIZE DATA CLIENT
 ↓
INITIALIZE REALTIME
 ↓
LOAD INITIAL SCREEN
 ↓
RUN
```

---

# 81. Authentication State

The application must distinguish:

```text
unknown
authenticated
unauthenticated
sessionExpired
authenticationError
```

Do not briefly render protected content before authentication state is known.

---

# 82. Session Expiration

If a session expires:

```text
active application
 ↓
session expired
 ↓
stop consequential commands
 ↓
preserve safe UI context
 ↓
reauthenticate
 ↓
reconcile state
```

---

# 83. Organization Context

If AEGIS supports multiple organizational contexts, the selected organization must be explicit.

Changing organization context must trigger appropriate:

```text
query invalidation
permission recalculation
realtime subscription update
navigation validation
```

---

# 84. Location Context

Location context should be handled explicitly where relevant.

Changing location filters must not silently change the user's operational organization or authorization scope.

---

# 85. Global State Architecture

The frontend global state should contain only cross-cutting concerns.

Conceptually:

```text
Global Application State
├── auth
├── organization
├── navigation
├── realtime
├── notifications
└── preferences
```

Domain server state remains in the server-state layer.

---

# 86. State Ownership Rule

Every piece of state must have one owner.

Ask:

> "Who is authoritative for this value?"

Possible answers:

```text
backend
application store
URL
component
form
```

Never allow two unrelated systems to independently own the same value.

---

# 87. Derived State

Derived state should be calculated rather than duplicated.

Example:

```text
threat.status
+
risk.score
+
investigation.status
```

may derive:

```text
availableActions
```

but available actions should ultimately respect backend permissions and domain rules.

---

# 88. Avoid Boolean Explosion

Do not create:

```text
isThreatActive
isThreatUnderReview
isThreatResolved
isThreatEscalated
```

all as independent mutable state.

Prefer the canonical domain status.

---

# 89. State Machines in UI

Where the domain state machine is important, frontend state should represent it explicitly.

Example:

```text
containment.status
```

rather than a collection of booleans.

---

# 90. Application Workflow State

Complex workflows may maintain temporary UI workflow state.

Example:

```text
Containment Workflow

review
→ authorization
→ confirmation
→ execution
→ verification
```

The workflow state must remain distinct from the authoritative backend containment state.

---

# 91. Unsaved Changes

The frontend must detect unsaved form changes where necessary.

Before navigation:

```text
unsaved draft
 ↓
warning
 ↓
continue / cancel
```

---

# 92. Browser Refresh

Refreshing a screen must not destroy authoritative domain state.

Durable state comes from:

```text
URL
+
API
+
authenticated context
```

not from temporary memory.

---

# 93. Offline Behavior

AEGIS should not pretend to support unrestricted offline operation.

If disconnected:

```text
read cached data where safe
display freshness state
disable unsafe commands
reconnect
reconcile
```

---

# 94. Security Boundaries

Frontend secrets must never include:

```text
database credentials
private API secrets
server-only signing keys
service credentials
```

Public runtime configuration must be distinguishable from server secrets.

---

# 95. Environment Architecture

At minimum:

```text
development
test
staging
production
```

must have separate configuration.

Never hard-code production endpoints into components.

---

# 96. Environment Variables

Configuration should be centralized.

Conceptually:

```text
API_BASE_URL
REALTIME_ENDPOINT
APPLICATION_ENV
PUBLIC_CONFIGURATION
```

Secrets remain server-side.

